        SimpleSal App software, or, SimpleSal software

=====================

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

SimpleSal software component README style guide (delete after copying master)

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

purpose of SimpleSal software existing in the first place:

SimpleSal is software with an introductory level of sophistication, with respect to the user's abilities.
The introduction is made through moderately complex software source code full of models and examples.

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

purpose of the software in this repository:

* Support the educational goals of the SimpleSal ecosystem by providing free software source code.
* Provide software source code of moderate complexity that can be broken, fixed, improved, abandoned.
* Demonstrate the benefits and costs of serious software engineering tasks of moderate complexity.

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

=====================

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

 contents of this repository:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

* What happens in my workspace as a result of interacting with this repository?

  * A design goal: any version of a SimpleSal App should work with any version of SimpleSal.
  * The App and the SimpleSal directories contain a version from a branch of their repositories.
  * The contents of the App's directory and the contents of SimpleSal are mutually exclusive.
    The result: App repositories without a SimpleSal directory, a SimpleSal repository with no App.

* for a non-SimpleSal App:   'the contents of the repository go into a directory with the name
                               required by the user's IDE, for Arduino, the ".ino" file name.'

* for a SimpleSal App:       'the contents.........".ino" file name.'   Same as non-SimpleSal.

* for the SimpleSal source:  'the contents, a SimpleSal directory, go into the App's directory.'

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

  * A design goal: no modifications to SimpleSal files are needed to modify the behavior or content.
  * Modifying an App and SimpleSal at the same time is complex, and also what SimpleSal is made for.
        Nothing in the repository organization blocks having both checked out at the same time.

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

=====================

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

 directory tree structure:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

* User space for Arduino is a directory "App" containing the "SimpleSal" directory and "App.ino".
  The Arduino IDE build environment expects a SimpleSal directory to be in each App's directory.

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

* the "SimpleSal" directory contains all of the software source code needed to use SimpleSal.

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

* the "ssTEA" subdirectory contains the software implementing Time and Event Agency (TEA).
  the "ssUI" subdirectory contains the menu system that displays Time and Event information.
  the "ssDocs" subdirectory contains documentation, each file is pointed to by source code files.
  the "ssIO" subdirectory is independent of ssTEA and implements the virtualization of hardware.

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

=====================

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

 directory tree content:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

  C~language source code at a "fundamental Kernighan and Ritchie C level"; also called "K\&R C".
  The C software is intended to directly translate to processor~level instructions and capabilities.
  The software is also going to be integrated into other languages and Hosts; we must begin at simple.
  This is a complete implementation, with a dependency for Time and Ascii on the platform or host OS.

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

  SimpleSal configuration and platform portability is implemented in files with the extension ".start".
  To begin using ssTEA, these files must be copied into the App user space and renamed as ".h" files.
  The files do not neeed to be changed; the directions for bringing SimpleSal into the App expect ".h".

  The expectation is that the normal user will extend the basic Mesa abilities and reconfigure ssTEA.
  The file system view of writability may remain from the SimpleSal directory clone from a repository.
  Instructions can be found in each file directly; each file points to additional ssDocs documentation.

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

  There is ssDocs documentation specific to the architecture and implementation of ssTEA and ssUI as
  a software component co~existing with the App.  For example, allocation of the database memory
  for Event descriptors may be performed by the App or ssTEA; the App is free to take control and
  replace ssTEA's static allocation algorithm with a dynamic allocation using malloc.  Try it.

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

